## D3. OpenMP reduction: sum 1 to 10

Save as `reduction.c`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <omp.h>

int main(void) {
    int sum = 0;

    #pragma omp parallel for reduction(+:sum)
    for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
        sum += i;
    }

    printf("Sum = %d\n", sum);
    return 0;
}
```

Compile and run:

```bash
gcc -fopenmp reduction.c -o reduction
./reduction
```

Expected result: `Sum = 55`.

## D4. OpenMP private

Save as `private.c`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <omp.h>

int main(void) {
    int x = 10;

    #pragma omp parallel private(x)
    {
        x = omp_get_thread_num();
        printf("Thread %d: x = %d\n", omp_get_thread_num(), x);
    }

    printf("Original x = %d\n", x);
    return 0;
}
```

Compile and run:

```bash
gcc -fopenmp private.c -o private
./private
```

Each thread gets its own initially uninitialized copy of `x`. We assign to that copy before reading it. The original `x` remains 10.

## D5. OpenMP firstprivate

Save as `firstprivate.c`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <omp.h>

int main(void) {
    int x = 10;

    #pragma omp parallel firstprivate(x)
    {
        x += omp_get_thread_num();
        printf("Thread %d: x = %d\n", omp_get_thread_num(), x);
    }

    printf("Original x = %d\n", x);
    return 0;
}
```

Compile and run:

```bash
gcc -fopenmp firstprivate.c -o firstprivate
./firstprivate
```

Each thread's private copy starts at 10; the original remains 10.

## D6. OpenMP lastprivate

Save as `lastprivate.c`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <omp.h>

int main(void) {
    int x = -1;

    #pragma omp parallel for lastprivate(x)
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        x = i;
        printf("Iteration %d, thread %d\n", i, omp_get_thread_num());
    }

    printf("Final x = %d\n", x);
    return 0;
}
```

Compile and run:

```bash
gcc -fopenmp lastprivate.c -o lastprivate
./lastprivate
```

Expected final line: `Final x = 3`. The printed iteration order can vary.

## D7. OpenMP critical

Save as `critical.c`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <omp.h>

int main(void) {
    int sum = 0;

    #pragma omp parallel for
    for (int i = 1; i <= 1000; i++) {
        #pragma omp critical
        {
            sum += i;
        }
    }

    printf("Total sum = %d\n", sum);
    return 0;
}
```

Compile and run:

```bash
gcc -fopenmp critical.c -o critical
./critical
```

Expected result: `Total sum = 500500`.

`critical` allows one thread at a time into the protected block. For a simple sum, `reduction(+:sum)` is often more efficient.

